# ============================================================================
#  profiles/full.env — the whole platform, nothing reduced
# ============================================================================
#  Sourced by `make up`. Deliberately almost empty: every stack's own .env
#  already holds the full-size values, so there is nothing to override.
#
#  The file exists anyway, because "full" should be a choice with a name
#  rather than the absence of one. It is also the place to put a value if the
#  full profile ever needs to differ from a stack's default.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  How these profiles work
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Sourced into the environment before Compose runs:
#
#      set -a; . ./profiles/full.env; set +a
#
#  Compose resolves ${VAR} from the process environment first and only falls
#  back to the .env beside the file it is reading. That is what makes one file
#  reach all eight stacks — and why this works where a --env-file would not.
#
#  A --env-file applies to the top-level compose file only. Each included
#  stack reads the .env in its OWN directory, which is the trap that left
#  Prometheus half-blind for five steps: a variable set in one stack was
#  invisible when the same service was started from another.
#
#  Variables a stack does not use are inert. Compose only interpolates names
#  that appear in the file it is processing, so 03-dbms never looks at
#  SPARK_WORKER_MEMORY even though it is in the environment.
# ============================================================================

# The ClickHouse topology, as a Compose profile. Eight nodes: four shards of
# two replicas, with three Keepers.
CLICKHOUSE_PROFILE=clickhouse
